      "math_academy_id": "q-184085",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=6{p}^{2}-8pq+2{q}^{2}+3,$ where $p,q\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6{p}^{2}-8pq+2{q}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =2(3{p}^{2}-4pq+{{field-2}})+1 \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=${{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $6{p}^{2}-8pq+2{q}^{2}+3:$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $3=2+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{odd}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6{p}^{2}-8pq+2{q}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =(6{p}^{2}-8pq+2{q}^{2}+2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{3{p}^{2}-4pq+{q}^{2}+1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=3{p}^{2}-4pq+{q}^{2}+1\\in Z.$",
